;;; A guard on a variable no premise before it binds read nothing, in
;;; silence — M1e2 **W24**, register row `W24/guard-before-binder`
;;; (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), banked by S1e2.2.24 (2026-11-05) from its
;;; step 1's probe `build/s1e2.2.24/shapes/g-order-rule.ein` (the program
;;; unchanged), ruled by the user on 2026-11-05 (Q-M1e2.18 (ii)(a)); restated
;;; by M1f S1f.5.7 on the user's ruling of 2026-11-07 (Q-M1e2.26, *premises
;;; should follow the same rule*), which supersedes it.
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Four facts of `p`, and two rules that write their guard before the
;;; premise that binds its variable: `(and (eq ?x a) (p ?x))` and
;;; `(and (neq ?x a) (p ?x))`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A rule's `:match` means the same in any order of its conjuncts
;;; (Q-M1e2.26), so each guard is judged after `(p ?x)`: `e` fires on `a`
;;; alone and `n` on `b`, `1` and `(f a)`. Root holds `(hit-eq a)`,
;;; `(hit-neq b)`, `(hit-neq 1)` and `(hit-neq (f a))`, its minimal line root's.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and why it does not any more ─────────────
;;;
;;; Until S1e2.2.24 the program loaded, and each guard compared `?x`, not yet
;;; bound, as a null, equal to no value: `e` never fired, and `n` fired for
;;; every `?x`, `a` included: four `hit-neq` facts and no `hit-eq`, exit 0,
;;; with no word of the order (`ein saturate --dump FILE`, `ein solve FILE`
;;; at `f8c10730`). From S1e2.2.24 to M1f S1f.5.7 the loader refused it,
;;; naming the variable (Q-M1e2.18 (ii)(a)), every command exiting 1 (`ein
;;; test`: 2). Since S1f.5.7's T1f.5.7.3 (`6841faff`, 2026-11-09) it loads and
;;; answers as above, which the register's row holds (`wrong_answers.rs`).
;;;

(relation p Node :why "p holds of {?1}") (relation q Node :why "q holds of {?1}") (relation f Node :why "f holds of {?1}") (relation hit-eq Node :why "hit-eq holds of {?1}") (relation hit-neq Node :why "hit-neq holds of {?1}")
(p a) (p b) (p 1) (p (f a)) (q a)
(rule e () :match (and (eq ?x a) (p ?x)) :assert (hit-eq ?x) :why "p holds of {?x}, which equals a, so hit-eq holds of {?x}")
(rule n () :match (and (neq ?x a) (p ?x)) :assert (hit-neq ?x) :why "p holds of {?x}, which is not a, so hit-neq holds of {?x}")
